    <!-- Help window (Shift+F1): command descriptions Help_Cmd_<Id> and guide articles Help_Topic_<Id> -->
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Copy">Copies the marked files (or the one under the cursor) to the other panel's folder. The dialog lets you edit the destination (for a single item a new name becomes the copy's name, a trailing \ makes it a folder), choose what happens when names clash, and set options: keep timestamps, only newer, parallel copy (an archive is read and written in one pass, so it has no threads). Works between the disk, archives and servers, and is recorded in the operation log.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Move">Moves the marked items to the other panel's folder, with the same dialog as Copy. A new name in the same folder works as a rename. The source is removed only after the item has safely arrived.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NewFolder">Creates a folder in the current folder and puts the cursor on it. Inside a ZIP archive it creates an empty folder in the archive; other archive formats are read-only. Not available in the search results list itself.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NewFile">Asks for a name, creates an empty file in the current folder and opens it for editing, like {gesture:Edit}; an existing file is simply opened. Works on disk and on servers, not inside archives.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Delete">Deletes the marked items (or the one under the cursor) after confirmation. On fixed local drives they go to the Recycle Bin; elsewhere (removable drives, network, servers, archives) they are deleted for good — the confirmation says which. Shows progress, with Pause and Cancel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_DeletePermanent">Same as Delete, but bypasses the Recycle Bin. It always asks first, and Enter in the question means Cancel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Rename">Renames the item under the cursor right in the list, as in Explorer: the name without the extension is preselected, Enter or Tab applies, Esc cancels; clicking another row or the other panel applies too. Rename in a file's context menu does the same. In the Tree view the name is asked in a window. To rename many files at once use Multi-rename ({gesture:MultiRename}): Rename in the menu of several marked files opens it too. Inside a ZIP archive the entry (a folder with everything in it) is renamed by rewriting the archive; other archive formats are read-only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Pack">Packs the marked items into an archive in the other panel's folder: ZIP, 7z (needs 7-Zip installed) or TAR, with a compression level and an optional password; can also add to an existing ZIP. Disk files only; junctions and folder links are left out (ZIP, TAR) and reported.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Unpack">Extracts the marked archives (or the one under the cursor) into the other panel's folder (the path can be changed), one after another; other marked files are passed over. For each it asks for the password, warns how many files would be overwritten and refuses unsafe paths inside the archive. To just look inside, press {gesture:OpenFile} on the archive.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_OpenFile">Enters a folder, opens an archive as a folder ({gesture:NavigateUp} at its root leaves it), or opens a file with the program Windows associates with it. A file inside an archive or on a server opens from a temporary copy, so edits to it are not saved back; use {gesture:Edit} to edit.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_View">Opens the file in the built-in viewer: highlighted code, Markdown, text, HEX or an image. It has an encoding choice, word wrap, find, block-by-block paging through huge files, and the N and P keys for the panel's neighbouring files. Works inside archives and on servers.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Edit">Opens the file for editing in the program set under Settings → Files → Editor, or, when none is set, with Windows' Edit action for its type (or Notepad). A file on a server opens through a temporary copy that is uploaded back on every save, checking that nobody changed the file meanwhile. Files inside an archive cannot be edited this way — extract them first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Properties">Shows the standard Windows Properties dialog for the item under the cursor. Disk files only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CalculateFolderSizes">Counts the size of every folder in the active panel and shows it in the Size column; a panel sorted by size is sorted again, the heaviest on top (node_modules, bin, .git). It runs in the background and works in archives and on servers too; links (junctions, symlinks) are not followed. "≥" before a size means some subfolders could not be read. {gesture:SelectToggleSize} counts one folder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SelectToggleSize">The same as {gesture:SelectToggle}, and a folder it marks is counted too: its size shows in the Size column and in the marked total in the status line (as Space does in Total Commander). All folders at once: {gesture:CalculateFolderSizes}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SelectToggle">Marks or unmarks the item under the cursor and moves down one row. Copy, delete and the other commands act on the marked files, or on the one under the cursor when nothing is marked.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SelectAll">Marks all visible files and folders (respecting the quick filter).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_DeselectAll">Clears all marks in the active panel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_InvertSelection">Inverts the marks: marked items are unmarked and the rest are marked (folders included).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SelectByMask">Marks items whose names match a mask, e.g. *.cs; *.md. Separate masks with ;, a comma or a space; *.* matches everything, folders included.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_UnselectByMask">Unmarks the items whose names match the mask.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ClipboardCopy">Puts the marked files on the clipboard to paste here ({gesture:ClipboardPaste}) or in Explorer. Works on disk, inside archives (those paste only within the app) and in search results; not on servers — use {gesture:Copy} there.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ClipboardCut">Like Copy to clipboard, but pasting moves the files; the originals are removed only after everything has arrived.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ClipboardPaste">Pastes files copied or cut here or in Explorer into the current folder (or into an open ZIP). Asks when names clash; a copy into the same folder becomes "file (1)".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CopyFullPath">Copies the full paths of the marked items as text, one per line; on ".." it copies the current folder's path.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CopyNames">Copies just the names of the marked items as text, one per line.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CopyReadLink">For files in S3 or Azure Blob: copies links anyone can open to download them, one per line, with no keys of their own. In the file menu the link lives an hour, a day or seven days; from the palette, an hour. The connection signs the link, so S3 needs an access key and Azure Blob the account key.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NavigateUp">Goes to the parent folder and puts the cursor on the folder you came from. While a quick filter is typed it deletes the filter's last character first. At the root of an archive, a server or search results it returns to the disk (a server connection is closed).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NavigateBack">Goes back to the previous folder in this panel's history (also the ◀ button left of the path). History starts over when you switch tabs or enter an archive or a server.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NavigateForward">Goes forward in the panel's history after Back (the ▶ button).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GoToRoot">Jumps to the root of the current drive (C:\ or \\server\share); inside an archive or on a server, to their root.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GoHome">Opens your Windows profile folder, leaving any open archive or server.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GoToClipboardPath">Opens the path on the clipboard: a folder directly, a file by opening its folder with the cursor on it. Surrounding quotes and spaces are ignored.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_EditPath">Turns the path bar of the active panel into a text box with the current path selected: type a folder (with suggestions) and press Enter, or Esc to leave it. A click on the empty part of the path bar does the same. With the path bar hidden in the settings, the path is asked in a box.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_OpenInExplorer">Shows the item under the cursor in Windows Explorer; with several marked items, or the cursor on "..", it opens the current folder. Disk files only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SwitchPanel">Makes the other panel active. Commands act on the active panel (its frame is highlighted); the other one is the target for copy and move.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ToggleSplit">Switches the panels between side by side and one above the other. The choice is remembered.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_FindFiles">Instant fuzzy search for a file by name and path within the project — from the git repository root, otherwise from the current folder; also inside archives. All space-separated words must match, Tab narrows to the highlighted folder, Enter takes the panel to the file. Files you pick often rank higher.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NameSearch">Instantly finds a file or folder by part of its name on all local drives, using a prebuilt index. The index is built in the background on first use and refreshed when it is older than 6 hours; system folders, .git and node_modules are left out.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_RebuildNameIndex">Rescans the drives for Find file anywhere — use it after files changed outside the app. Runs in the background; running it again during a rebuild offers to cancel it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_FolderTree">Shows or hides a tree of drives and folders to the left of the panels. Clicking a folder opens it in the active panel; the tree follows the active panel to its folder. It updates itself from what the panels show; {gesture:RefreshTab} re-reads all its open folders.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_QuickFilter">Opens the active panel's quick filter without typing the first letter (like Total Commander's quick search key); if it is open, gives it the keyboard back. The matching part of each name is highlighted, Enter opens the one under the cursor, Esc closes the filter. The Tree view has no filter: letters go to the folder whose name starts with them.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ToggleHiddenFiles">Shows hidden and system files in the active panel, or hides them again. The setting is remembered per panel. The same switch is a right-click on the view buttons in the panel's status line and in the column header's menu.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ToggleExtensions">Shows file names in the active panel with or without their extensions (in Details the extension can have a column of its own). The setting is remembered per panel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ToggleFoldersFirst">Keeps folders above files whatever the sort order, or mixes them with the files by the chosen column. The setting is remembered per panel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ToggleIgnored">Hides the files and folders excluded by .gitignore (bin, obj, node_modules…) in the active panel, or shows them again. Works inside a git working copy; the setting is remembered per panel — if familiar folders seem to be missing, check it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ColumnSettings">Chooses the columns of the Details view: which to show, their order and width; applies to both panels. Among them are line count (LOC), size on disk and Git — the file's status in the repository.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ViewModeDetails">Shows the active panel as a table: name, size, date and the other columns chosen with {gesture:ColumnSettings}; click a header to sort. Each panel keeps its own view, and the buttons above the list switch it too.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ViewModeBrief">Shows the active panel as a compact list of names with icons: the names run top to bottom in columns, and the list scrolls sideways (the mouse wheel too). Up and Down move within a column, Left and Right to the next column, Page Up and Page Down by a screenful of columns; with Shift the keys mark the files on the way and keep the marks made with Insert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ViewModeThumbnails">Shows the active panel as tiles. Pictures on a local disk (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, TIFF, ICO; WebP, HEIC and AVIF where Windows has the codec) get a preview, turned upright the way the camera recorded it; other files, archives, servers and cloud files that are not downloaded keep their icon. The arrow keys move the cursor across the tiles, Home and End go to the first and the last, and with Shift they mark the files on the way.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ViewModeTree">Shows the active panel as a tree of drives and folders: selecting a folder opens it, selecting a file puts the cursor on it in its folder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NewTab">Opens a new tab on the current folder in the active panel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CloseTab">Closes the current tab (also the × button or a middle-click). A locked tab and the last remaining tab cannot be closed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NextTab">Switches to the next tab of the active panel, wrapping around.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_PreviousTab">Switches to the previous tab of the active panel, wrapping around.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_RefreshTab">Re-reads the active panel's listing, keeping the cursor and marks, and refreshes the git branch on the tab. On disk the panel usually updates itself; this is for archives, servers and after git commands run in a terminal. In the Tree view, and in the folder tree when it is shown, it also re-reads the drives and every open folder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_DuplicateTab">Creates a copy of the tab right next to it, with the same folder and name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CloseOtherTabs">Closes all of the panel's tabs except the current one; locked tabs stay.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ToggleLockTab">Locks the tab to its folder (lock icon): it cannot be closed, and whenever you return to it, it reopens that folder. Run it again to unlock.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_RenameTab">Gives the tab a name of its own instead of the folder name (also double-click the tab). An empty name restores the folder name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Search">File search: by name mask or regular expression, by content, size and attributes, including inside archives and honouring .gitignore; criteria can be saved as presets. Show in panel lists the hits in the active panel, where the usual commands work on them. Searches local disks.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_MultiRename">Batch rename with a template ([N] name, [E] extension, [C] counter, [D] and [T] date and time, [P] folder), regular-expression replace, inserting and deleting characters, and case changes. The preview shows conflicts straight away; after Rename you can press Undo while the window is open. Inside a ZIP archive the whole batch is one rewrite of the archive, all or nothing; other archive formats are read-only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_OperationLog">History of file operations, yours and agents': copy, move, delete, rename, create, pack and extract, downloads and uploads (the transfer queue and saves of a file opened from a server), with result and errors. A line can be saved as a reusable operation. The one thing the log can undo is a rename batch of AI rename (Undo rename); for the rest the way back is the Recycle Bin.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_BackgroundOperations">Shows again the progress windows hidden with the In background button. A copy, move or delete keeps running while its window is hidden; the status bar shows how many operations run in the background and how far they have got, and a click there does what this command does. Operations with a server on one side run beside those on the disk, so a long download does not hold up a local copy.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_AddBookmark">Adds the active panel's current folder to the bookmarks under a name you choose. Bookmarks are in the Bookmarks menu.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_BookmarksManager">Bookmark editor: name, path, order and group (groups become submenus of the Bookmarks menu), import and export as JSON. Changes apply on OK.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Terminal">Opens a terminal in the active panel's folder: Windows Terminal if installed, otherwise PowerShell or cmd. Disk folders only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_OpenInEditor">Opens the current folder and the marked files in your default editor (VS Code, Cursor, Rider, Zed, Sublime Text, Notepad++ and others are found automatically). Choose the default under Settings → External tools, or with Open in editor….</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_OpenInEditorPicker">Like Open in editor, but lets you pick the editor from a list first; your pick becomes the default.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_RunAgentHere">Starts an agent CLI (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Aider and others) in a new terminal window in the active panel's folder. The launch is recorded as an agent session, so its edits show up later in Agent changes ({gesture:AgentChanges}).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_RunAgentPicker">Like Run agent here, but lets you choose the agent first; the choice is remembered.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_RunGadget">A gadget is a saved, named command line, e.g. build = dotnet build. This opens a searchable list of gadgets and runs the chosen one in the active panel's folder. Gadgets are added, edited and deleted under Settings → Gadgets (Commands → Manage gadgets…); while there are none, the command offers to describe the first one in one line.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GadgetsManager">Opens Settings on the Gadgets tab: add, edit and delete gadgets — name, command, arguments with macros, working folder, and whether it runs in a terminal. A gadget is picked by its name, so names must be unique.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ShellsManager">Opens Settings on the Shells tab: the shells the command line at the bottom of the window runs commands in — cmd, PowerShell, Git Bash, WSL or one of your own: name, program, and arguments where %C is the typed command. The shell for the next command is picked left of the command line.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SavedOperations">A saved operation is a named copy, move, delete or create-folder you repeat by picking it from a list. Files are taken from the active panel by mask, and the target may use macros (%O is the other panel's folder). Operations are added, edited and deleted in Manage saved operations… (Commands menu); Save as an operation… in the operation log ({gesture:OperationLog}) turns a logged copy or move into one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SavedOperationsManager">The list of saved operations: add, edit, rename and delete them. For each you choose the action (copy, move, delete, create a folder), which files of the active panel it takes (masks, or the marked files), the target with macros, and whether it asks before running; moving and deleting always ask. Changes are written on OK.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_DropStackAdd">The drop stack is a temporary basket: collect files from several folders into it, then copy or move them all at once. This command adds the marked files. The stack lasts until you close the program.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_DropStackShow">Shows the drop stack: remove entries, clear it, or copy or move the ticked files into the active panel's folder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CompareDirectories">Compares the left and right panels' folders (with subfolders; disk, archive or server) and shows what is missing on one side, which file is newer and which differs. The arrow on each row sets the direction; Synchronise copies the ticked rows after a confirmation. Nothing is deleted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Checksums">Computes checksums of the marked files (MD5, SHA1, SHA256, SHA512, CRC32), saves them to a file and, on the Verify tab, checks files against such a file. Disk files only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_UserToolsManager">User tools are your own menu items that start a program with arguments. Each has a command, arguments with macros (%P folder, %S marked files, %N name, %E extension, %T the other panel's folder), a working folder and a shortcut. They appear in the Commands menu, in the palette and on their own key.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_EditRemoteFile">Edits a file on a server: downloads a temporary copy, opens it for editing and uploads it back after every save; if the server copy changed meanwhile, it asks first. {gesture:Edit} on a server panel does this for you.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitDiff">Shows the changes of the file under the cursor against the last commit, staged and unstaged together. On a folder it shows everything inside it, on ".." the current folder. Read-only; the diff can be copied.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitWorktrees">Every worktree of the repository: branch, state (clean, has changes, locked, folder missing), ahead/behind as of the last fetch, and path. From here you can open a worktree, create one or remove one — removing deletes the folder with its uncommitted work (the branch stays), so it asks first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitNewWorktree">Creates a worktree for a task: the task title becomes a branch and a folder next to the repository, local config files (.env and similar) are copied in, a setup command (npm install, dotnet restore) can run, and a workspace of its own is created.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitCommit">Commit window: changes and the index, buttons to stage and unstage, diff, and reverting selected files to the last commit (confirmed first). You can commit all tracked changes at once. With a language model configured, AI draft writes the message from the diff.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitBranch">Local branches: switch, create one from the current commit, or delete one. With uncommitted changes, switching offers to stash them first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitStage">Adds the marked files (or the one under the cursor) to the git index, so they go into the next commit. Folders are added as a whole.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitUnstage">Takes the marked files out of the index; the files on disk are unchanged.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitStash">Puts all uncommitted changes, new files included, into the stash and leaves the working tree clean. The description is optional. The stash is shared by all worktrees of the repository.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitStashPop">Restores the latest stash entry and drops it. It shows which entry that is first; on a conflict the entry is kept.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_GitClone">Clones a repository from an https URL into the panel's current folder; the URL is taken from the clipboard. For a private repository enter a user name and token — the token is kept in memory only. SSH is not supported: an SSH link to GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket or Codeberg is cloned over https instead, any other SSH link needs its https URL.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_AgentChanges">Every uncommitted change in the repository, with who made it and how sure that is: an agent write you approved, an operation of this app, "probably an agent", or unknown. Any change reverts in one click (the file is backed up first). The same window has the operation log, agent sessions, MCP server controls and the folders agents may reach.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_AgentLaunch">Starts an agent on a task: optionally creates a separate worktree with its own branch and workspace, records a session (its edits then show up in Agent changes) and opens the agent in a terminal. The task text goes to the clipboard — paste it as the agent's first message.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_AiRename">Proposes readable names for "junk" files (scans, downloads, photos) from their content, using your language model. Nothing is renamed until Apply, names can be edited, and Undo works in the window and, after it is closed, in the operation log ({gesture:OperationLog}). Does not work inside a git repository or on code. Needs a model under Settings → Agent layer.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SemanticSearch">Search by meaning: a question in words ("where is the token checked?") finds matching code fragments. Press Index first — file text is sent to the embedding model, and the index stays on this machine. Needs an embedding model under Settings → Agent layer.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ConnectionManager">Saved connections: SFTP (password or key), FTP and FTPS, WebDAV, S3 and compatible storage (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO, Wasabi, Yandex…), Azure Blob, Google Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive and Windows shares. On the left, the list with search and a mark where a connection is open; on the right, its fields: edit, test, colour tag, read-only. An address pasted at the top fills the fields. Passwords, keys and sign-in tokens are stored encrypted for your Windows account. Import adds the hosts of ~/.ssh/config as SFTP connections, after showing what it will add; the ones already saved are left out.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Disconnect">Closes the server connection (in the active panel, or in the other one if the active panel has none) and returns the panel to the disk folder it showed before.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_QueueTransfer">Queues the marked files for a background transfer between a server and the disk: a download if the server panel is active, an upload from disk to the server in the other panel otherwise. Transfers can be paused and resumed; files only, not folders.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_TransferQueue">The transfer queue window: per-file progress, pause, resume, cancel, retry after an error and clearing finished items. It does not block the panels; the queue lasts until you close the program.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceSwitcher">A searchable list of saved workspaces: type part of a name and press Enter, and both panels with their tabs and layout return to that project's state. The group and a "pinned" mark are shown on the right.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_NewWorkspace">Creates an empty workspace. A window asks for the name, icon, colour and group, then both panels open in your home folder with one tab each. The workspace you were on is saved, as on any switch. The new workspace is pinned to the workspace sidebar straight away (untick it in the window if you prefer). Dropping a folder on the workspace sidebar opens the same window, and then both file panels open in that folder. To copy the current panels under a new name, use {gesture:SaveWorkspace}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_SaveWorkspace">Saves the current state of both panels (tabs, folders, view, layout) as a workspace. The active workspace's name is filled in, so Enter just updates it; another name creates a new one, pinned to the workspace sidebar. To start from empty panels, use {gesture:NewWorkspace}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceNotes">Notes for the workspace (Markdown) plus the command and working folder this project is usually started with — e.g. npm run dev. The notes are kept as a file next to the program.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceTerminal">Opens a terminal in the workspace's working folder and runs the command from its notes (with no command, just a terminal). A command that came from someone else's project file is shown and confirmed first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceNewWindow">Opens the workspace in a separate window of the program, to keep two projects side by side. That window does not change the shared settings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceSidebar">Shows or hides the workspace sidebar on the left: the pinned workspaces (by group) and the one open now. Click a workspace to switch projects; right-click it for its actions. Drag a workspace up or down to reorder it within its group; a folder dropped on the sidebar becomes a new workspace.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceOpenDefinition">Opens a .tcvproj project file — a workspace description you can keep in the repository: folders, colour, group, terminal command. Paths are relative to the file, so after a clone it is enough to open it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceExportDefinition">Saves the workspace as a .tcvproj file next to the project, to commit it and open it on another machine. Only the shareable parts go in: folders, appearance, terminal command.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Workspace1">Switches straight to the workspace with that number in the list. Pinned workspaces come first, so they get the first numbers.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceProperties">One window for the active workspace's name, icon, colour, group and pinning (any other one through the Workspaces menu). Pick a colour from the set or the system picker; the group is a heading above the workspace on the workspace sidebar; the preview shows the result straight away.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspacePin">Pins or unpins the active workspace. A pinned one is always on the workspace sidebar and comes first in the list (getting {gesture:Workspace1}, {gesture:Workspace2}…); the sidebar turns itself on when you pin. An unpinned one shows there only while it is open.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_WorkspaceDelete">Deletes the workspace together with its notes (after confirmation). The project's folders and files are not touched.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CommandPalette">The command palette: every command of the program and of your tools, with the current keys. Start typing a name (optionally with its category, e.g. "tab close"); Enter runs the highlighted command. The connections opened last come first (“Connect: name”): Enter opens one in the active panel.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Help">This window: a guide to the program and every command with its key, menu location and description. Run executes the command in the main window, and the help stays open.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Settings">All program settings: appearance and language, which parts of the window are shown, file-operation behaviour, colour rules, editors and agents, worktrees, gadgets, the MCP server and the language model.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_HotkeySettings">Commands and their keys: find a command, press a new shortcut (or a two-stroke chord) and apply it. Clashes with other commands are refused; Reset restores the default key.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_PluginManager">Shows the loaded plugins (file systems, tools, columns) and loads a new .NET plugin from a DLL. A plugin is foreign code, so load only trusted ones.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_CheckForUpdates">Checks GitHub for a newer version. A portable copy installs it itself: it downloads only what changed, closes and opens again as the new version, and settings and workspaces stay. A dev build or an old ZIP offers the download page instead. The program goes online only when you run this.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ReportBug">Opens the bug report form on GitHub with the version and Windows already filled in. Describe what happened and attach the log (Help → Open the log folder). The program sends nothing by itself: you see the form and submit it in your browser.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Discussions">Opens the project's discussions on GitHub: questions, ideas and how you use the program. Bugs go through Report a bug.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_OpenLogFolder">Shows the program's logs in Explorer with the newest one selected: attach it to a bug report. The logs stay on this computer; a new file starts every day and the last seven are kept.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ExportSettings">Saves settings, workspaces, search presets and user tools into one .tcvsettings file, to move them to another machine. The model key, MCP server, approved folders, connections and notes are not included.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_ImportSettings">Replaces the current settings, workspaces, search presets and user tools with the contents of a .tcvsettings file (after confirmation); most changes apply at once.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Quit">Closes the program, saving the panels and the current workspace's layout. If copying or a transfer is still running, it asks first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Cmd_Menu">Opens the main menu from the keyboard; then use the arrow keys and Enter.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Start">Getting started</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Start_Body" xml:space="preserve">## Two panels&#10;The window has two file panels. The active one has its tab underlined and a dot in its status line: commands act on it, and the other panel is the target for copy and move. Switch with {gesture:SwitchPanel} or a mouse click.&#10;• The cursor is the row you are on. Marks are the files chosen for an operation: {gesture:SelectToggle} marks a file and moves down, {gesture:SelectToggleSize} does the same and also counts a folder's size, {gesture:SelectAll} marks everything. A click with Ctrl marks or unmarks one file and leaves the rest; a click with Shift and the arrows with Shift mark a run, and the arrows with Shift started on a marked file unmark instead. With nothing marked, commands act on the file under the cursor.&#10;• Start typing to open the quick filter: the list keeps the names containing the text, and the cursor lands on the best match (a name that starts with it, then a word inside a name). ↑ and ↓ step through the matches, {gesture:OpenFile} opens the one found, and {gesture:View}, {gesture:Copy} and the other commands work on it right from the box; Esc clears the filter. Text with * or ? is a mask of the whole name: *.cs is C# files only.&#10;• {gesture:OpenFile} enters a folder or opens a file, {gesture:NavigateUp} goes up a level.&#10;## Buttons at the bottom&#10;They follow classic Total Commander: {gesture:View} view, {gesture:Edit} edit, {gesture:Copy} copy, {gesture:Move} move, {gesture:NewFolder} new folder, {gesture:Delete} delete, {gesture:Terminal} terminal, {gesture:Quit} exit. {gesture:Rename} renames; the menu opens with Alt.&#10;## The command line&#10;Under the panels is the command line. cd path moves the active panel; anything else runs in a terminal in the panel's folder, in the shell picked left of the line: cmd, PowerShell, Git Bash, WSL. Add shells of your own in Commands → Command-line shells….&#10;## Finding any command&#10;• {gesture:CommandPalette} is the command palette: start typing a name, Enter runs it. It holds everything the program can do, with the current keys.&#10;• {gesture:Help} is this help: what every command does and where it is in the menus.&#10;• Any key can be reassigned: Settings menu → Keyboard shortcuts.&#10;## What the program is for&#10;It is built for working next to LLM agents: see quickly what an agent changed ({gesture:AgentChanges}), look at the diff ({gesture:GitDiff}), roll back what you do not want, and switch between projects instantly — workspaces ({gesture:WorkspaceSwitcher}).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Panels">Panels, tabs and finding files</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Panels_Body" xml:space="preserve">## Path and drives&#10;• Above each panel are the drive list and the path bar. Click part of the path to go there; click the empty part of the bar, or press {gesture:EditPath}, to type a path with suggestions. Inside an archive or on a server the first part is its name (archive.zip, sftp://user@host) and leads to its root.&#10;• On a network share (\\server\share) the drive list shows the share instead of a drive. The list follows drives as they come and go (USB, mapped network drives).&#10;• {gesture:NavigateBack} and {gesture:NavigateForward} move through the panel's history.&#10;• {gesture:GoToRoot} goes to the drive root, {gesture:GoHome} to your profile folder, {gesture:GoToClipboardPath} to the path on the clipboard.&#10;## Tabs&#10;• Each panel has its own tabs: {gesture:NewTab} new, {gesture:CloseTab} close, {gesture:NextTab} next. Double-click a tab to rename it, middle-click to close it, right-click for a menu (duplicate, lock, close others). When the tabs do not fit, the mouse wheel scrolls them and the ▾ button on the right lists them all.&#10;• The current git branch is shown next to the tab's name.&#10;## View&#10;• View buttons in the panel's status line: Details (columns), Brief, Thumbnails, Tree. Right-click them for hidden files ({gesture:ToggleHiddenFiles}), extensions and folders first; the same switches are in the Settings menu and the column header's menu. The views also have keys: {gesture:ViewModeDetails}, {gesture:ViewModeBrief}, {gesture:ViewModeThumbnails}, {gesture:ViewModeTree}.&#10;• Home and End go to the first and last row in every view; with Shift they mark everything on the way, or unmark it when started on a marked row.&#10;• Sort by clicking a column header. Choose columns with {gesture:ColumnSettings}; the Git column shows each file's status.&#10;• {gesture:ToggleIgnored} hides files excluded by .gitignore (bin, obj, node_modules). {gesture:FolderTree} shows a folder tree on the left. {gesture:ToggleSplit} puts the panels side by side or one above the other.&#10;## Finding a file&#10;• Letters typed in the list open the quick filter, and {gesture:QuickFilter} opens it before the first letter: the list narrows to the names that match, the matching part of each name is highlighted, and Enter opens the one under the cursor. Settings → Files turn it into a jump: the list stays whole, the cursor goes to the match, ↑ and ↓ go to the next ones. Text that matches nothing turns red. In the Tree view letters go to the visible folder whose name starts with them.&#10;• {gesture:FindFiles} — by name within the current project: fuzzy and instant.&#10;• {gesture:NameSearch} — by name on all drives, from an index.&#10;• {gesture:Search} — the full search: by mask, content, size, inside archives; hits can be listed in a panel.&#10;• {gesture:SemanticSearch} — by meaning, using a language model.&#10;## Archives and search results&#10;{gesture:OpenFile} on an archive opens it as a folder. ZIP can be changed; other formats are read-only. Search hits listed in a panel are real files: you can copy, delete, rename, pack and edit them, show them in Explorer and run tools on them, but nothing can be created in that list. Tools, editors and agents started from the list work in the folder it was opened over. Leave an archive or the results with {gesture:NavigateUp} at their root.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Files">File operations and undo</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Files_Body" xml:space="preserve">## Copy and move&#10;{gesture:Copy} and {gesture:Move} send the marked items to the other panel's folder. The dialog lets you change the destination and choose what happens when names clash. Operations run in the background with progress, Pause and Cancel; an error on one file offers retry, skip or abort.&#10;## Delete&#10;On a local disk (search hits included) {gesture:Delete} sends files to the Recycle Bin; elsewhere (removable drives, network, servers, archives) it deletes for good — the confirmation always says which. {gesture:DeletePermanent} always bypasses the Recycle Bin.&#10;## Clipboard and drag-and-drop&#10;• {gesture:ClipboardCopy}, {gesture:ClipboardCut} and {gesture:ClipboardPaste} work on files and interoperate with Explorer.&#10;• {gesture:CopyFullPath} copies paths as text, {gesture:CopyNames} copies names.&#10;• Files can be dragged between the panels and to and from Explorer; hold Shift or drag with the right mouse button to move. Files of a server or cloud panel go to the other panel the same way. Entries of an open archive dropped on the other panel are extracted there, and dropped on the ".." row at the archive's root, into the folder the archive lies in.&#10;## The log, and what can be undone&#10;• Every operation — yours and agents' — is written to the log ({gesture:OperationLog}).&#10;• There is no general undo. The ways back are: the Recycle Bin for what was deleted from a local disk; Undo in Multi-rename while its window is open; Undo in AI rename, and later Undo rename in the log; reverting files to the last git commit ({gesture:AgentChanges}, {gesture:GitCommit}).&#10;## Repeatable actions&#10;• {gesture:SavedOperations} — saved operations, created from the log or in Commands → Manage saved operations….&#10;• {gesture:DropStackAdd} collects files from several folders into a stack; {gesture:DropStackShow} copies or moves them all at once.&#10;• {gesture:MultiRename} renames by a template, {gesture:CompareDirectories} compares and synchronises two folders.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Workspaces">Workspaces</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Workspaces_Body" xml:space="preserve">## What it is&#10;A workspace is the saved state of a whole project: both panels with all their tabs and folders, the view and the layout. Switching to a workspace brings everything back as it was — faster than tabs and bookmarks.&#10;## Create, save, switch&#10;• {gesture:NewWorkspace} creates an empty workspace: both panels in your home folder. The one you were on is saved.&#10;• {gesture:SaveWorkspace} saves the current state under a name (or updates the active workspace).&#10;• {gesture:WorkspaceSwitcher} is a searchable list of workspaces; {gesture:Workspace1}, {gesture:Workspace2}… jump straight to the workspace with that number.&#10;• When you leave a workspace (switch away or close the program), its current tabs are saved automatically.&#10;## Properties: name, icon, colour, group&#10;{gesture:WorkspaceProperties} opens one window with all the active workspace's properties; for any other one use the Workspaces menu → the workspace → Properties.&#10;• The icon is an emoji shown before the name in the menu, the switcher and the window title; on the workspace sidebar it stands in place of the two letters.&#10;• The colour is a strip under the menu, the active panel's frame and a dot on the workspace sidebar. Pick one from the set or press Other…. It tells windows of different projects apart.&#10;• The group is a heading above its pinned workspaces on the workspace sidebar and a label in the switcher, e.g. "Work" and "OSS". Workspaces without a group come first, then the groups from A to Z. A group only matters once many workspaces are pinned.&#10;## The workspace sidebar and pinning&#10;The workspace sidebar on the left shows the pinned workspaces and the one open now. A pinned workspace is always there and comes first in the list. An unpinned one shows only while it is open: it sits under a rule after the pinned ones and leaves the sidebar when you switch away. A new workspace is pinned straight away. To pin or unpin, right-click the workspace on the sidebar or use the Workspaces menu → the workspace; the sidebar turns itself on when you pin. Hide or show it with {gesture:WorkspaceSidebar}.&#10;## Order and a new folder&#10;• You set the order: drag a workspace up or down the sidebar, within its group. The numbers {gesture:Workspace1}, {gesture:Workspace2}… count down the sidebar, and the menu and the switcher list the workspaces in the same order. A workspace just pinned or moved to another group goes to the end of it.&#10;• A folder dropped on the sidebar (from a file list or Explorer) becomes a new workspace: the properties window opens, and then both file panels open in that folder. If the folder is already open in a panel of some workspace, you simply switch to it.&#10;## Notes and terminal&#10;{gesture:WorkspaceNotes} holds notes for the project and the command it is started with (npm run dev, dotnet watch…). {gesture:WorkspaceTerminal} opens a terminal in the project folder and runs that command.&#10;## Several windows and the project file&#10;• {gesture:WorkspaceNewWindow} opens a workspace in a separate window.&#10;• {gesture:WorkspaceExportDefinition} saves the workspace description as a .tcvproj file you can commit; {gesture:WorkspaceOpenDefinition} opens such a file — for example after a clone.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Git">Git</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Git_Body" xml:space="preserve">## Seeing the state&#10;• The current branch is shown on the tab next to its name.&#10;• The Git column ({gesture:ColumnSettings}) shows each file's status in git status --short letters: red conflict, amber modified, green staged, grey untracked.&#10;• {gesture:GitDiff} shows the diff of the file or folder under the cursor.&#10;## Recording changes&#10;{gesture:GitStage} and {gesture:GitUnstage} add the marked items to the index and take them out, {gesture:GitCommit} opens the commit window, {gesture:GitBranch} the branches, {gesture:GitStash} and {gesture:GitStashPop} put changes away and bring them back. Push and pull are deliberately left out — use the terminal for them.&#10;## A worktree for a task&#10;A worktree is a second working folder of the same repository on another branch. It is a handy way to give an agent its own copy of the project: {gesture:GitNewWorktree} creates a worktree with a branch, copies local config files and creates a workspace; {gesture:GitWorktrees} lists all worktrees and removes the ones you no longer need.&#10;## Cloning&#10;{gesture:GitClone} clones a repository from an https URL; if it contains a .tcvproj file, the program offers to open it as a workspace.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Agents">Working with agents</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Agents_Body" xml:space="preserve">## Starting an agent&#10;• {gesture:RunAgentHere} starts an agent (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Aider and others) in a terminal in the current folder; {gesture:RunAgentPicker} lets you choose the agent.&#10;• {gesture:AgentLaunch} starts an agent on a task, optionally in a separate worktree with its own branch and workspace.&#10;• A launch from the program is recorded as a session: everything that changes under its folder is marked "probably an agent". Close the session when the agent is done, or your own edits will be attributed to it too.&#10;## Review and roll back&#10;{gesture:AgentChanges} shows every uncommitted change in the repository and who made it. You can view a change (diff), stage it or revert it; before a revert the file is backed up into the agent-revert-backups folder next to the program.&#10;## The MCP server: the agent sees the panels&#10;• The MCP server lets an agent read what is open in the panels: the selection, file lists, contents (including inside archives), search and the operation log.&#10;• Turn it on under Settings → Agent layer or on the MCP server tab of Agent changes, which also has the registration command for the agent. The server listens on 127.0.0.1 only and requires a token.&#10;• An agent reaches only the folders you approved. If agents may propose changes, every copy, move or delete from an agent comes to an approval window, with Refuse as the default.&#10;## Language model&#10;AI rename ({gesture:AiRename}), semantic search ({gesture:SemanticSearch}), the commit message draft and the viewer's summary use your model (Ollama, LM Studio or a cloud one). Set the address and models under Settings → Agent layer; while the address is empty, the program contacts nothing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Remote">Servers and cloud</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Remote_Body" xml:space="preserve">## Connections&#10;The Connections button on the drive strip connects this very panel: its list holds the saved connections (the open ones ticked), Quick connect ({gesture:QuickConnect}) and the manager ({gesture:ConnectionManager}). An address such as sftp://user@host/path or s3://bucket can also be typed straight into the path bar.&#10;While the panel is on a connection, the button shows its name, colour tag and a state dot: green, the server answers; amber, a listing is loading; red, the server did not answer (Reconnect is then in its menu). When the connection drops, the panel reconnects by itself — after 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 and then 30 seconds, eight tries in all — and the list shows the countdown with Retry now and Stop reconnecting; a refused login or a changed server key is not retried. The connections of background tabs are kept alive, so they are still open when you come back. Disconnect ({gesture:Disconnect}) is beside it.&#10;## Tabs&#10;A connection lives in the tab it was opened in: the tab is named after it, and going to another tab and back does not close it. Another server opens in a new tab; one already open is shown where it is. Closing the tab closes the connection; switching workspaces closes them all.&#10;## Files on a server&#10;• {gesture:Copy} and {gesture:Move} copy between the server and the disk, {gesture:View} shows a file in place.&#10;• {gesture:Edit} edits a file through a temporary copy: every save is uploaded back.&#10;• {gesture:QueueTransfer} queues files for a background transfer that can be paused and resumed; the queue window is {gesture:TransferQueue}.&#10;• Deleting on a server is permanent (Google Drive and OneDrive move the file to their own bin).&#10;• A read-only connection lets nothing be changed — neither by you nor by an agent.&#10;## Cloud storage&#10;S3 and compatible storage (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO, Wasabi, Yandex Object Storage, Google Cloud Storage with HMAC keys) connects with an access key or an AWS profile; picking the provider fills in the endpoint and region. Azure Blob uses the account key or a SAS token. Google Drive, Dropbox and OneDrive sign in through the browser and need the client ID of an app registered with the provider.&#10;## Security&#10;On the first SFTP connection the program shows the server's key fingerprint and asks whether to trust it. If the key changes later, the connection is refused — it may mean interception; if the change is legitimate, use Forget server key in the connection manager. Passwords, keys and sign-in tokens are stored encrypted for your Windows account.&#10;## Windows network folders&#10;Paths like \\server\share open when typed into the path bar; mapped network drives are in the drive list. Save a share that needs its own login in the manager as a Windows share (SMB).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Customize">Customising</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help_Topic_Customize_Body" xml:space="preserve">## What is set up where&#10;• Settings ({gesture:Settings}): theme and language, which parts of the window are shown, delete and copy behaviour, colour rules for files, editors and agents, command-line shells, worktrees, gadgets, the MCP server and the language model.&#10;• Keys: Settings menu → Keyboard shortcuts. A two-stroke chord can be assigned too.&#10;• Your own commands: user tools (Commands menu) are menu items that start a program with arguments; gadgets ({gesture:RunGadget}) are named command lines.&#10;• Moving to another machine: {gesture:ExportSettings} and {gesture:ImportSettings}.&#10;## Where the data lives&#10;The program is portable: settings, workspaces, notes, the log, connections and plugins live in its folder; the registry and AppData are not used. The folder can be moved as a whole; passwords and the model key are encrypted for your Windows account and must be re-entered on another machine.</sys:String>
